      title: "Письма, на которые отвечают",
      text: "The average office worker receives more than a hundred emails a day, so it is hardly surprising that many messages are ignored, misunderstood or answered weeks late. Yet some people seem to get quick, helpful replies almost every time. What are they doing differently?\n\nThe first secret is the subject line. A recipient often decides within a second whether to open a message, so 'Question' or 'Hello' is a wasted opportunity. Compare that with 'Invoice 4512 overdue: payment needed by Friday'. The second version tells the reader what the email is about, why it matters and what is expected, before they have even opened it. If the message is time-sensitive, say so clearly, but use the word 'urgent' sparingly: people quickly learn to ignore colleagues for whom everything is urgent.\n\nThe second secret is structure. Effective business emails put the main point or request in the first two sentences. Background information, if it is needed, comes afterwards. Long blocks of text should be broken into short paragraphs, and if you are asking several questions, number them. That way the reader can answer point by point instead of replying to the first question and forgetting the rest.\n\nTone is the third, and perhaps most difficult, area. Emails lack the facial expressions and intonation that soften spoken language, so a sentence that sounds neutral in your head may seem cold or even rude on the screen. 'You haven't sent the report' feels like an accusation; 'I haven't received the report yet' simply states a fact. Even when you are chasing an overdue invoice, a polite but firm approach usually works better than an angry one. A gentle reminder can be followed by a firmer follow-up email, and only then should you escalate the matter to a manager.\n\nFinally, always proofread before you press 'send'. Check that the attachment is really attached, that you have spelled the recipient's name correctly, and that you have not copied in anyone who should not see the message. It takes thirty seconds and can save you a very embarrassing apology.",
