    text: "When offices reopened after the pandemic, many companies assumed that meetings would simply go back to normal. Instead, a new and often frustrating format appeared: the hybrid meeting, in which some people sit together in a conference room while others join from home. For many teams it has turned out to be the worst of both worlds.\n\nThe problems are familiar to anyone who has taken part in one. People in the room chat among themselves and forget about their remote colleagues, whose faces appear in small boxes on a screen at the end of the table. Microphones pick up the sound of coffee cups but not of the person speaking quietly in the corner. Remote participants find it almost impossible to chip in, because by the time they have unmuted themselves, the conversation has moved on. 'I sometimes leave a meeting with the feeling that it must have taken place without me,' says one software developer who works from Lisbon for a company based in Berlin.\n\nSome organisations have responded with surprisingly simple rules. At one consulting firm, it is the principle of 'one person, one screen' that has made the biggest difference: even colleagues who are in the office join from their own laptops, so that everyone appears in the same way and has the same chance to speak. Others appoint a 'remote champion', a person whose only job during the meeting is to make sure online participants are heard. Many teams now share the agenda and any documents in advance, and the chair regularly invites remote colleagues to comment by name.\n\nThere is also a growing recognition that not every discussion needs to be a meeting at all. 'What most teams really need is fewer meetings, not better technology,' argues Dr Helen Grant, who studies workplace communication. She suggests that updates should be shared in writing, leaving meetings for decisions and for the kind of creative brainstorming that genuinely benefits from conversation.\n\nHybrid work is unlikely to disappear. However, the companies that handle it well seem to share one attitude: they treat it not as a temporary problem but as a skill that can be learned.",
